// NEGATIVE TEST -- a top-level item may not have a `String` type (N3-09).
//
// A Palladium `String` is a pointer into a 64 KiB arena that the runtime sets
// up, so its value is produced by code that runs -- and nothing runs before
// `main`. The type set for a top-level item is therefore closed and small:
// the integer types, `f32`, `f64` and `bool`, each of which C can initialise at
// file scope with no code at all.
//
// This fixture pins the TYPE refusal rather than the initialiser refusal: the
// initialiser is an integer literal, which is a perfectly good constant
// expression, so the only thing wrong with the item is the type it declares.
const NAME: String = 1;

fn main() {
    print(NAME);
}
